%N A398171 a(n) is the number of positive integers that are less than or equal to and relatively prime to exactly one divisor of n.
%C A398171 There is a beautiful identity: Sum_{d|n} phi(d) = n, where phi is the Euler totient function.
%C A398171 We can rewrite this identity as the cardinality of a set of pairs. Define P(n)={(d,x) in N^2 : d|n, 1<=x<=n, gcd(x,n)=1} and we have that |P(n)| = n. For example, |P(6)| = |{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(3,2),(6,1),(6,5)}| = 6.
%C A398171 We might be interested in the frequencies of a certain x value of P(n). Let r(n,y)=|R(n,y)| where R(n,y)={(d,x) in P(n) : x=y}. For example, r(6,1) = |{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(6,1)}| = 4 while r(6,2)=r(6,5)=1 and for other x values r(6,x) = 0. This gives yet another reformulation of the identity: Sum_{x=1..n} r(n,x) = n. For example, Sum_{x=1..6} r(6,x) = 4+1+0+0+1+0 = 6.
%C A398171 This sequence counts the number of 1's in each such sum. Define a(n)=|Q(n,1)| where Q(n,y) = {x in [1,n] : r(n,x)=y}; that is, Q(n,y)={x in N : |{d in N : d|n, x<=d and gcd(x,d)=1}|=y} or specifically Q(n,1) = {x in N : there is a unique d|n, x<=d and gcd(x,d)=1}. For example a(6) = |Q(6,1)| = |{2,5}| = 2.
%e A398171 a(6) = |{2,5}| = 2 because 2 is less than and relatively prime to 3 but no other divisor of 6, and 5 < 6 | 6.
%e A398171 a(10) = |{2,4,7,9}| = 4 since 2 is relatively prime to 5 (but not less than 1 and not relatively prime to itself or 10) and 4 < 5 and 7,9 < 10.
%o A398171 (Python)
%o A398171 from math import gcd
%o A398171 def a(n):
%o A398171     divs=[d for d in range(1,n+1) if n%d==0]
%o A398171     Qn1=[]
%o A398171     for x in range(1,n+1):
%o A398171         Rnx=[d for d in divs if gcd(x,d)==1 and x<=d]
%o A398171         if len(Rnx)==1:
%o A398171             Qn1.append(x)
%o A398171     return len(Qn1)
%o A398171 (PARI) a(n) = my(d=divisors(n)); sum(i=1, n, sum(j=1, #d, (i<= d[j]) && (gcd(i, d[j])==1)) == 1); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Jul 23 2026
